{"records":[{"id":"49gxpjrcn9","title":"Craniofacial cartilage organoids from human neural crest stem cells","description":"We hypothesize that knowledge of cell signaling pathways that drive human neural crest differentiation into craniofacial chondrocytes may be obtained using human stem cells that self-organize into organoids. We developed a differentiation protocol that generated self-organizing craniofacial cartilage organoids from human embryonic stem cell-derived neural crest stem cells. Histological staining of cartilage organoids revealed tissue architecture and staining typical of elastic cartilage. Protein and post-translational modification (PTM) mass spectrometry and snRNA-seq data showed that chondrocyte organoids expressed robust levels of cartilage extracellular matrix (ECM) components: many collagens, aggrecan, perlecan, proteoglycans, and elastic fibers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-04T09:52:28.414Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/49gxpjrcn9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/49gxpjrcn9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mark","last_name":"Grimes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Craniofacial Biology","Neural Crest","Human Pluripotent Stem Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5vh2dvxkb6","title":"Original western blot images-YTHDC1","description":"The original western blot images showed in the paper 'YTHDC1 Safeguards Human Embryonic Stem Cell Viability via Transcriptional Control of Survival Genes'.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-09T08:19:24.538Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5vh2dvxkb6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5vh2dvxkb6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ying","last_name":"Jin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3twnpm7bdd","title":"Promoter-Enhancer Communication Occurs Primarily within Insulated Neighborhoods","description":"Esrrb was shown previously to interact directly with Mediator in mouse embryonic stem cells (mESCs) and Esrrb depletion does not lead to mESC differentiation. Based on these facts, we used Esrrb knockdown (Esrrb KD) as a proxy for enhancer inactivation, because Esrrb KD removes Mediator from the enhancers. These Western blots show that Esrrb indeed recruits Mediator to DNA template and Mediator plays a key role in assembling pre-initiation complex (PIC). For details, refer to the paper and description of each figure. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-10-18T20:45:31.637Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3twnpm7bdd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3twnpm7bdd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fei","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"r4t3vv372f","title":"B Catenin is a downstream mediator of Pten and glycolysis","description":"Uncropped western blot data published in the article entitled: \"Glycolytic flux controls retinal progenitor cell differentiation via regulating Wnt signaling\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-21T08:15:40.586Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r4t3vv372f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r4t3vv372f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yacine","last_name":"Touahri","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carol","last_name":"Schuurmans","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Retina","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65k3y4smr5","title":"ATG5 attenuates inflammatory signaling in embryonic stem cells to control differentiation","description":"Attenuated inflammatory responses to both inflammation-related cytokines and infectious agents are recognized as a unique property of embryonic stem cells (ESCs). Although an increasing number of studies have reported this attenuation, the underlying molecular mechanisms are still unclear. Moreover, whether the attenuated inflammatory status is involved in ESC differentiation is completely unknown. Here, we found that the autophagy-related protein ATG5 is essential for both the attenuated inflammatory response and the differentiation of ESCs, and that the attenuation of inflammatory signaling is required for ESC differentiation. Mechanistically, ATG5 promotes 26S proteasome-mediated degradation of the SCF (SKP1-cullin-1-F-box-protein) E3 ubiquitin ligase β-TrCP1 (beta-transducin repeat containing E3 ubiquitin protein ligase 1), which is a well-known positive regulator of NF-κB signaling and the inflammatory response. Another SCF E3 ubiquitin ligase, FBXW7 (F-box and WD repeat domain containing 7), which is recruited by ATG5, was found to be the E3 ubiquitin ligase responsible for the ubiquitination and degradation of β-TrCP1. Moreover, the differentiation defects observed in ATG5-depleted ESCs are due to the accumulation of β-TrCP1 and the related hyperactivation of NF-κB signaling, as loss of β-TrCP1 and inhibition of NF-κB signaling rescued the differentiation defects in ATG5-depleted ESCs. Therefore, this study reveals a previously uncharacterized mechanism employed by ESCs to maintain the inflammatory response in an attenuated state and further provides novel insight into the biological roles of ATG5, especially in the control of the inflammatory response and differentiation of ESCs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-22T08:57:47.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65k3y4smr5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65k3y4smr5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bo-Wen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying-Cui","last_name":"Wan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bei-Bei","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian-Ru","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Fan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia-Ni","last_name":"Shang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen-Ge","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yan","last_name":"Xi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Su","last_name":"Chen","orcid":"0000-0001-8804-8989"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"ZR2021QC181"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"222300420028"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82273067"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81972650"}],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kk8jr9k6nn","title":"Data for \"B1 lymphocytes develop independently of Notch signaling during mouse embryonic development\"","description":"B1 lymphocytes are a small but unique component of the innate immune-like cells. However, their ontogenic origin is still a matter of debate. Although it is widely accepted that B1 cells originate early in fetal life, whether or not they arise from hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) is still unclear. In order to shed light on the B1 cell origin, we set out to determine whether their lineage specification is dependent on Notch signaling, which is essential for the HSC generation and, therefore, all derivatives lineages. Using mouse embryonic stem cells (mESCs) to recapitulate murine embryonic development, we have studied the requirement for Notch signaling during the earliest B-cell lymphopoiesis and found that Rbpj-deficient mESCs are able to generate B1 cells. Their Notch independence was confirmed in ex vivo experiments using Rbpj-deficient embryos. In addition, we found that upregulation of Notch signaling induced the emergence of B2 lymphoid cells. Taken together, these findings indicate that control of Notch signaling dose is crucial for different B-cell lineage specification from endothelial cells and provides pivotal information for their in vitro generation from PSCs for therapeutic applications.\nDOI: 10.1242/dev.199373","source":{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-09T14:37:22.876Z","url":"https://ordr.hsr.it/datasets/kk8jr9k6nn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kk8jr9k6nn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Ditadi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rebecca","last_name":"Scarfò","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Momoko","last_name":"Yoshimoto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Natalia","last_name":"Azevedo Portilho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elisa","last_name":"Bertesago","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ismail","last_name":"Ismailoglu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Kyba","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michihiro","last_name":"Kobayashi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Embryonic Development of the Immune System","Notch","Hematopoietic Development","Embryonic Development","Lymphopoiesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wc7f54nc5d","title":"Riboregulation of Enolase 1 activity controls glycolysis and embryonic stem cell differentiation. Huppertz et al.","description":"Differentiating stem cells must coordinate their metabolism and fate trajectories. We report here that the catalytic activity of the glycolytic enzyme enolase 1 (ENO1) is directly regulated by RNAs leading to metabolic rewiring in mouse embryonic stem cells (mESC). We identify RNA ligands that specifically inhibit ENO1’s enzymatic activity in vitro and diminish glycolysis in cultured human cells and mESCs. Pharmacological inhibition or RNAi-mediated depletion of the protein deacetylase SIRT2 increases ENO1’s acetylation and enhances its RNA binding. Similarly, induction of mESC differentiation leads to increased ENO1 acetylation, enhanced RNA binding and inhibition of glycolysis. Stem cells expressing mutant forms of ENO1 that escape or hyper-activate this regulation display impaired germ layer differentiation. Our findings uncover acetylation-driven riboregulation of ENO1 as physiological mechanism of glycolytic control and of the regulation of stem cell differentiation. Riboregulation may represent a more widespread principle of biological control.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-16T21:59:04.823Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wc7f54nc5d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wc7f54nc5d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ina","last_name":"Huppertz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joel I.","last_name":"Perez-Perri","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Panagiotis","last_name":"Mantas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thileepan","last_name":"Sekaran","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Schwarzl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francesco","last_name":"Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dunja","last_name":"Ferring-Appel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zuzana","last_name":"Koskova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lyudmila","last_name":"Dimitrova-Paternoga","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eleni","last_name":"Kafkia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Janosch","last_name":"Hennig","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pierre A.","last_name":"Neveu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kiran","last_name":"Patil","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthias","last_name":"Hentze","orcid":"0000-0002-4023-7876"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics","Gas Chromatography Mass Spectrometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tsk65tw2kx","title":"Supporting data on Combined transcriptomic and phosphoproteomic analysis of BMP4 signaling in human embryonic stem cells","description":"Dataset supports the development of a flexible system for the culture and differentiation of human embryonic stem cells that relies on the commercially available mTeSR without select factors medium, supplementing with Activin A instead of TGFb.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-29T17:49:14.721Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tsk65tw2kx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tsk65tw2kx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Angelos","last_name":"Papadopoulos","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Embryonic Stem Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xyv5tj2rcs","title":"EIF3D orchestrates the homeostasis of multiple signaling pathways governing the primed pluripotent stem cell identity","description":"Pluripotent stem cell identities such as differentiation and infinite proliferation have long been decoded in the frameworks of transcription factor networks, epigenomes, and signal transduction, yet unclear and fragmented. However, directing attention toward translation regulation, the bridge between these events promises to provide new insights into previously unexplained mechanisms. Functional CRISPR interference screening led to the discovery that EIF3D maintains primed pluripotency via selective translation regulation. The loss of EIF3D unbalanced the pluripotency-associated signaling pathways, disrupting primed pluripotency. Furthermore, we found that EIF3D safeguards robust proliferation by managing the translation of multiple p53 regulators that maintain low p53 activity in the undifferentiated state. Therefore, this study provides a paradigm for selective translation regulation that defines the primed pluripotent stem cell identity.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-30T14:04:26.679Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xyv5tj2rcs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xyv5tj2rcs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kazutoshi","last_name":"Takahashi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Numerical Analysis","Stem Cell","Flow Cytometry","Photograph"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r5db9w567v","title":"Lipid Metabolism Remodeling in Human Cardiomyocyte Differentiation and Maturation","description":"In this study, the low-input trace lipidomics approach was utilized to analyze the dynamic changes of lipidomics during the differentiation process of human cardiomyocytes.Overall, our findings indicate that lipid species undergo a transition: from simple hydrocarbons with lower saturation in early embryonic stem cells to more complex lipids with longer hydrocarbon chains and higher unsaturation in later stages during CM differentiation. The simple lipid species in the early stage are suited for rapidly assembling basic membrane structures and serving as raw materials for synthesizing complex lipid molecules. In contrast, the complex lipid species in the later stage are crucial for maintaining membrane stability and fluidity during CM contraction; they can also act as second messengers to participate in more complex cellular signaling.\nFurthermore, the remodeling of the lipidome during this process partly reflects the metabolic transition from a glycolysis-dominated stem cell state to mature CMs reliant on oxidative phosphorylation. Current research remains in the basic stage. Future studies can build on the current findings regarding lipid unsaturation and specific lipid changes during cardiac differentiation to investigate related regulatory genes, further clarifying their regulatory mechanisms and deeper physiological roles. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-23T08:17:18.202Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r5db9w567v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r5db9w567v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haite","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raina","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiqiang","last_name":"Xiong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiqi","last_name":"He","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gang","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wai-Yee","last_name":"Chan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wuming","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Strategic Seed Funding for Collaborative Research Scheme from CUHK","grant_id":"#MK/WW/SSFCRS2324/8014/24jh"},{"id":null,"name":"Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC)-Young Scientists Fund","grant_id":"#82200314"},{"id":null,"name":"Direct Grant for Research from CUHK","grant_id":"#2021.069 and #2022.082"},{"id":null,"name":"Innovation and Technology Commission of the Hong Kong Government grants ","grant_id":"GHX09721SZ and MHP00523"},{"id":null,"name":"the National Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC)/Research Grants Council (RGC) Joint Research Scheme ","grant_id":"#N_CUHK428/22"},{"id":null,"name":"the Chinese University of Hong Kong (CUHK)–Shandong University (SDU) Joint Laboratory on Reproductive Genetics","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cell Differentiation","Lipidomics","Cell Metabolism","Embryonic Stem Cell","Cardiomyocyte"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122951,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":107,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122951,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":122479,"restricted":60,"embargoed":412},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2062},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1334},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":583},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":387},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":378},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":339},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":279},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":256}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":120381},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1891},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":177},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":126},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":69},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":59},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":873},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":762},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":400},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":386},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":357}]}}